WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.130
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.770
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:19.001
Unit forty-two: The Ethics of Journalism. In this unit, you will evaluate ethical dilemmas in journalism with careful stance.

4
00:00:20.017 --> 00:00:21.668
journalistic ethics.

5
00:00:22.368 --> 00:00:26.875
journalistic ethics means the moral principles that guide reporters and editors.

6
00:00:27.175 --> 00:00:31.683
Journalistic ethics require reporters to verify before publishing.

7
00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:35.882
Your turn. Say: journalistic ethics.

8
00:00:38.582 --> 00:00:40.129
journalistic ethics.

9
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:42.315
public interest.

10
00:00:43.015 --> 00:00:48.591
public interest means what benefits society as a whole, rather than mere curiosity.

11
00:00:48.891 --> 00:00:52.330
Exposing corruption is clearly in the public interest.

12
00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:56.425
Your turn. Say: public interest.

13
00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:00.405
public interest.

14
00:01:01.417 --> 00:01:02.940
privacy intrusion.

15
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.832
privacy intrusion means unjustified entry into someone's private life.

16
00:01:09.132 --> 00:01:13.326
Photographing grieving families can be a serious privacy intrusion.

17
00:01:14.026 --> 00:01:18.083
Your turn. Say: privacy intrusion.

18
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:22.446
privacy intrusion.

19
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.765
source protection.

20
00:01:25.465 --> 00:01:31.005
source protection means keeping the identity of confidential informants secret.

21
00:01:31.305 --> 00:01:34.628
Without source protection, whistleblowers would stay silent.

22
00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:38.561
Your turn. Say: source protection.

23
00:01:41.261 --> 00:01:42.506
source protection.

24
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:45.087
balance and fairness.

25
00:01:45.787 --> 00:01:50.944
balance and fairness means presenting different sides of a story accurately.

26
00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:55.635
Balance and fairness do not mean treating lies and facts equally.

27
00:01:56.335 --> 00:02:00.300
Your turn. Say: balance and fairness.

28
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.558
balance and fairness.

29
00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.190
sensational coverage.

30
00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:13.198
sensational coverage means reporting that exaggerates to shock or excite audiences.

31
00:02:13.498 --> 00:02:16.995
Sensational coverage of crime increases public fear.

32
00:02:17.695 --> 00:02:21.207
Your turn. Say: sensational coverage.

33
00:02:23.907 --> 00:02:25.570
sensational coverage.

34
00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:28.118
embedded journalism.

35
00:02:28.818 --> 00:02:34.011
embedded journalism means reporters travelling with military units during a conflict.

36
00:02:34.311 --> 00:02:38.087
Embedded journalism gives access but may limit independence.

37
00:02:38.787 --> 00:02:42.008
Your turn. Say: embedded journalism.

38
00:02:44.708 --> 00:02:46.127
embedded journalism.

39
00:02:47.117 --> 00:02:48.675
press accountability.

40
00:02:49.375 --> 00:02:56.228
press accountability means the responsibility of media to correct errors and answer for their reporting.

41
00:02:56.528 --> 00:03:00.629
Press accountability includes publishing corrections promptly.

42
00:03:01.329 --> 00:03:05.177
Your turn. Say: press accountability.

43
00:03:07.877 --> 00:03:09.319
press accountability.

44
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:12.583
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:12.883 --> 00:03:17.437
Rithy, should a newspaper publish a politician's private messages?

46
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.551
It would seem that it depends on the public interest.

47
00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:23.665
Go on.

48
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.910
If the messages reveal corruption, publication is arguably justified.

49
00:03:31.217 --> 00:03:33.948
And if they only reveal his love life?

50
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.956
Then it is, to my mind, a clear privacy intrusion.

51
00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.993
Some editors claim that any scandal sells.

52
00:03:44.283 --> 00:03:50.265
Admittedly it does, but that tends to suggest profit, not ethics, is driving the decision.

53
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.012
Grammar focus: Hedging and stance (extended).

54
00:03:55.312 --> 00:03:57.765
Hedging and stance show how certain you are.

55
00:03:58.065 --> 00:04:03.097
It would seem that it depends on the public interest. Publication is arguably justified.

56
00:04:03.397 --> 00:04:08.264
Revealing a love life is, to my mind, a clear privacy intrusion.

57
00:04:08.564 --> 00:04:16.009
Mix hedging and firm stance deliberately: hedge where evidence is uncertain, commit where your principle is clear.

58
00:04:17.017 --> 00:04:20.784
Now read and listen. Truth, Harm and the Headline.

59
00:04:21.084 --> 00:04:26.276
Journalism is sometimes called the first draft of history, and its ethics matter accordingly.

60
00:04:27.583 --> 00:04:33.786
Journalistic ethics typically ask reporters to seek truth, minimise harm and act independently.

61
00:04:35.083 --> 00:04:40.740
The public interest can justify revealing secrets, but mere curiosity arguably cannot.

62
00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:47.683
Privacy intrusion is particularly troubling when victims of tragedy are involved.

63
00:04:48.983 --> 00:04:54.152
Source protection, meanwhile, allows whistleblowers to expose wrongdoing safely.

64
00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:01.652
Balance and fairness are essential, although giving equal time to falsehoods would seem to mislead audiences.

65
00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:07.840
Sensational coverage attracts clicks but tends to distort public understanding.

66
00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:16.595
Press accountability, through corrections and independent councils, helps preserve trust in a free media.

67
00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:20.559
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.859 --> 00:05:24.345
What benefits society as a whole is the blank.

69
00:05:24.645 --> 00:05:30.643
A, public interest. B, privacy intrusion. C, sensational coverage.

70
00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.369
The answer is A: public interest.

71
00:05:38.883 --> 00:05:41.324
Which phrase is a tentative hedge?

72
00:05:41.624 --> 00:05:47.135
A, It would seem that. B, It is certain that. C, Obviously.

73
00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.614
The answer is A: It would seem that.

74
00:05:55.117 --> 00:05:58.335
Which phrase expresses explicit personal stance?

75
00:05:58.635 --> 00:06:03.241
A, to my mind. B, it is said that. C, apparently.

76
00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.400
The answer is A: to my mind.

77
00:06:10.917 --> 00:06:14.240
Keeping informants' identities secret is blank.

78
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:20.701
A, source protection. B, embedded journalism. C, press accountability.

79
00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.668
The answer is A: source protection.

80
00:06:29.183 --> 00:06:33.006
According to the text, what does sensational coverage tend to do?

81
00:06:33.306 --> 00:06:40.221
A, distort public understanding. B, increase accuracy. C, protect sources.

82
00:06:44.217 --> 00:06:47.714
The answer is A: distort public understanding.

83
00:06:49.217 --> 00:06:56.107
Now it's your turn. Part one: When, if ever, is it right for journalists to publish private information?

84
00:07:01.407 --> 00:07:05.926
Part two: Hedge where you are unsure and take a firm stance where you are certain.

85
00:07:12.226 --> 00:07:23.391
It would seem that the key test is the public interest. Revealing corruption is arguably justified. Publishing someone's private family life, however, is to my mind simply wrong.

86
00:07:23.691 --> 00:07:26.189
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:27.183 --> 00:07:32.169
Great work today! You learned eight new words and hedging and stance (extended).

88
00:07:32.469 --> 00:07:36.445
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:36.745 --> 00:07:40.315
See you in Unit forty-three. Goodbye!

90
00:07:40.615 --> 00:07:43.186
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

